o stars on a federal rating scale  which looks in part at staffing and  health inspections nursing home  operators cut staffing to maximize  profit and when you don't have enough  staff it's simply not possible to have  good infection control dark says  lawsuits are one way of forcing change  and holding facilities accountable it is  very possible that a wave of lawsuits  could ultimately wipe out a significant  portion of long-term care profession  mark Regan is general counsel for the  California Association of health  facilities one of six health care groups  asking California's governor to issue an  executive order granting immunity  we have caregivers who are doing their  absolute best every day against  impossible odds and to allow the  second-guessing of those caregivers  would be really unfortunate agree who  has now hired an attorney himself hopes  this devastating outbreak can bring  meaningful change in senior care we  don't want this to happen to anybody  else because this was just wrong and we  can now report the Alameda County  District Attorney's Office is conducting  a criminal investigation into gateway  the owners of the facility reportedly  own at least seven other nursing homes  they've not returned our calls for  comment nursing home advocacy group say  state inspectors and nursing homes  should take infection control violations  more seriously  and they say immunity would undermine  that effort Anthony a truly devastating  situation there in California Jonathan  thank you
 

